  "nameStory": "The orange mycena gets its common name from its vibrant orange coloration that makes it stand out like a tiny beacon in the forest. It's also called Lea's mycena, honoring Thomas Gibson Lea, a 19th-century American botanist who collected specimens in Ohio and helped document many fungi species.",
  "description": "Orange mycena is a small, eye-catching mushroom that grows in dense clusters on rotting hardwood logs throughout New England. These delicate fungi have bright orange caps and stems that seem to glow against the dark bark they call home. The entire mushroom typically measures just 1-2 inches tall, making them easy to overlook despite their vivid color. They're saprophytes, meaning they help break down dead wood and return nutrients to the forest ecosystem.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Bright orange, bell-shaped to conical, usually 1/2 to 1 inch across with a smooth, slightly sticky surface when fresh",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills",
      "description": "White to pale yellow with distinctive reddish-orange edges that create a beautiful contrast against the pale gill faces",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Slender, hollow, and bright orange matching the cap, often appearing translucent and fragile to the touch",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Pattern",
      "description": "Always grows in dense, crowded clusters on deciduous logs, never alone or scattered",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Mild, slightly earthy odor without any strong or unpleasant scents",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Orange mycena thrives on decaying hardwood logs and stumps in deciduous and mixed forests throughout New England. Look for them especially on oak, maple, and beech logs in moist, shaded areas where the wood stays consistently damp. They prefer lower elevations and are commonly found along forest trails, in woodlands, and near streams where humidity levels remain high.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Early clusters may appear on logs as temperatures warm and spring rains provide moisture, though they're less common than in fall.",
    "summer": "Present during humid periods and after summer rains, particularly in shaded forest areas where logs stay moist despite warmer temperatures.",
    "fall": "Peak season for orange mycena, when they fruit abundantly on logs as autumn moisture and cooler temperatures create ideal conditions.",
    "winter": "Rarely visible in New England winters, though they may occasionally appear during warm spells in southern regions."
  },
  "funFact": "The orange pigment in these mushrooms contains natural antibiotic compounds, essentially making them tiny orange medicine cabinets growing on forest logs!",
  "confusionNotes": "Orange mycena might be confused with other small orange mushrooms, but the combination of dense clustering on logs, reddish gill edges, and bright orange coloration throughout makes them distinctive. Remember: \"Orange clusters on logs with red-rimmed gills\" - no other small New England mushroom shares all these features.",
  "contextualKnowledge": null,
  "compareHints": "Among the bonnet family (Mycena), orange mycena is unique for its vibrant orange coloration - most other bonnets are brown, gray, or white. While Bleeding Fairy Helmet also has reddish features, it's much darker overall and bleeds red latex when cut. The clustered growth pattern also sets orange mycena apart from most other bonnets, which typically grow individually or in small groups rather than dense masses.",
